$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/ai-foundations/checking-ai-changes

::checking-ai-changes o1 - Claim against diff::An assistant's summary says "Adds a log line and keeps the welcome message." Which line in the diff contradicts the second claim? {
=A removed line, - echo Welcome to pep #Correct. Removing the welcome line shows the opposite of keeps the welcome message, which is why removed lines need reading too.
~An added line, + echo Log started #Not quite. This line supports the first claim, adds a log line; it says nothing against keeping the welcome message.
~A removed test line for the welcome message #Not quite. A deleted test weakens what a passing run proves, but it is not the line that shows the message itself changed.
~None, because the summary already says the message is kept #Not quite. A summary is claims, not evidence; only the diff and your runs can support or contradict it.
}

::checking-ai-changes o2 - From supported to verified::You read a diff and find a line that matches the summary's claim "prints the chosen name." The story has two acceptance criteria. You have not run anything yet. What should you do to reach a verified result? {
=Run both acceptance criteria and compare each output with the expected result #Correct. A supported claim is still unverified; only runs whose output matches the expected result make it verified.
~Nothing, because a matching diff line already verifies it #Not quite. A matching diff line makes a claim supported, but it is still not run, so it is not verified.
~Ask the assistant to confirm that it ran the checks #Not quite. The assistant's answer is another claim; you need your own matching runs.
~Run only the first criterion, since one pass shows the change works #Not quite. Every acceptance criterion must be run and match before the result is verified.
}

::checking-ai-changes o3 - Decide with evidence::An AI-made change to greet.sh says "Shorter code, same output." Your run of AC2 expects Hello, friend but prints only Hello, and the diff removes the line that set friend as the default name. Which review decision is best? {
=Request changes, citing the removed default-name line and the failed AC2 run, and ask for the default to be restored #Correct. A good decision names its evidence and what to change.
~Approve, because AC1 passed and the summary says the output is the same #Not quite. A failed AC2 run contradicts the summary, so the change is not verified.
~Request changes with the comment "this looks wrong" #Not quite. The decision is right, but it cites no specific line, failed check or fix.
~Request changes only because an assistant wrote it #Not quite. Who wrote the change does not decide it; the evidence does.
}
